<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tabriz</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tabriz is a city in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, historically one of the principal commercial and political centres of the region. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> it became a major hub of trade where Mongol, Persian, Armenian and European merchants met. In 1501 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a> captured Tabriz and proclaimed himself Shah, making it the first capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>, though it was later repeatedly contested with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tabriz.jpg" alt="Tabriz" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elmju · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tabriz_Municipality_Sa%27at_Tower.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The confederation also controlled important stretches of the caravan routes that carried Iranian raw silk westwards to Bursa and Aleppo, and <b>Tabriz</b>, its capital after 1469, was one of the great commercial cities of western Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Mongols made <b>Tabriz</b> the capital of the Ilkhanate in the second half of the thirteenth century, Trebizond became a principal Black Sea outlet for the route from Persia, through which silk, spices and other goods reached Western merchants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Hulagu made Maragha in Azerbaijan his capital, later supplanted by <b>Tabriz</b>, and adopted the title “Ilkhan”, usually understood as a subordinate khan acknowledging the supremacy of the Great Khan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Constitutionalists held out in <b>Tabriz</b> under leaders such as Sattar Khan, and in July 1909 forces from Gilan and the Bakhtiari tribes took Tehran and deposed the shah in favour of his young son Ahmad Shah.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the fourteenth century they lived in the shadow of the Jalayirid Sultanate of Baghdad and <b>Tabriz</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…fourteen-year-old Ismail I, head of the Safaviyya Sufi order based at Ardabil in north-western Iran, led the Qizilbash, a confederation of Turkmen tribes (traditionally seven) named for their distinctive red headgear, to victory over the Aq Qoyunlu at the Battle of Sharur, seized <b>Tabriz</b> and proclaimed himself Shah.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
